Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>5  A toolbar</H1>
<HR>
RISC OS programs put the things you do most often where you can reach them with one click: Draw's toolbox,
Paint's tools, the button bars of word processors. In this chapter !Contacts gets a toolbar across the top of
its window, with buttons for New, Change and Delete, and a Find field that shows only the contacts whose names
contain what you type. The toolbar is the fourth toolkit module, <CODE>Toolbar</CODE>, which the organiser will
use again for its toolbox in Part Two. The result is <CODE>!Contacts5</CODE>:<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/C5Toolbar" WIDTH=629 HEIGHT=409></CENTER>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>Panes</H2>
A toolbar has to stay put while the list scrolls under it, and move when the window moves. RISC OS does this
with a <I>pane</I>: a second window, with no title bar or scroll bars of its own, that the Wimp keeps
directly in front of the main window and moves with it. Draw's toolbox is a pane, and so is the toolbar of
!JsEdit, the editor you may be reading this beside.<P>
The desktop does the work. You make the pane as an ordinary window with <CODE>flags: { pane: true }</CODE>,
and then attach it to the main window:<P>
<PRE>
win.attachPane(pane, { dx: 0, dy: 0, h: 44, fitWidth: true });
</PRE>
<CODE>dx</CODE> and <CODE>dy</CODE> say where the pane sits, measured from the top-left of the main window's
visible area; <CODE>fitWidth</CODE> makes it as wide as the window, whatever size the user drags it to.
From then on the pane opens, closes, moves and comes to the front with the window. It covers the top of the
window's work area, so whatever is drawn there must start lower down - you'll see how the list does that in a
moment.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The Toolbar module</H2>
<CODE>Toolbar</CODE> builds such a pane from a list of buttons. Its header comment says how to use it:<P>
<PRE>
// Toolbar - a row of buttons across the top of a window, like the
// toolbars of RISC OS programs, or a column of them down its left
// side, like Draw's toolbox. It is a pane: a window of its own that
// the Wimp keeps in front of the main window, and moves with it.
//
//   const bar = new Toolbar(w, {
//     area,                           // the program's own sprites
//     buttons: [{ name: 'new', sprite: 'tb_new', help: 'New card',
//                 action: () =&gt; ... }, { gap: true }, ...],
//     search: { help: '...', change: (text) =&gt; ... },   optional
//     menu: (ev) =&gt; ...,              Menu clicked on the toolbar
//     side: 'left', width: 56,        optional: a toolbox down the
//   });                               left instead (buttons can be
//                                     { text: 'Week', h: 26 } too)
//   bar.height / bar.width            how much of the window it hides
//   bar.shade('delete', true)         grey a button out (or back)
//   bar.setText(name, text), bar.setSprite(name, sprite)
//   bar.hide(name, true)              take a button away (or back)
//   bar.search                        the search field's icon
</PRE>
Each button is an icon in the pane, with a picture (a sprite), a 3D border, and a name, so it can be found
again with <CODE>iconByName</CODE>. The icons are made in a loop, one after another along the top (or down
the side, for a toolbox like Draw's), and the pane is made from them:<P>
<PRE>
    const size = down ? { w: this.width, h: win.h }
      : { w: win.w, h: this.height };
    this.pane = win.task.createWindow({
      flags: { pane: true }, colours: { workBg: 1 }, spriteArea: area,
      extent: down ? { w: this.width, h: Math.max(y, 2000) }
        : { w: 2000, h: this.height },
      ...size, icons,
    });
</PRE>
When a button is clicked, the pane looks up its action by the icon's name and calls it - unless the button is
greyed out:<P>
<PRE>
    this.pane.on('click', (ev) =&gt; {
      const f = this.actions.get(ev.icon?.name);
      if (f &amp;&amp; !ev.icon.shaded &amp;&amp; ev.button !== 'menu') f(ev);
      if (ev.button === 'menu') menu?.(ev);
      return true;
    });
</PRE>
and <CODE>shade</CODE> greys a button out or brings it back:<P>
<PRE>
  shade(name, on) {
    const icon = this.icon(name);
    if (icon &amp;&amp; icon.shaded !== !!on) icon.setState({ shaded: !!on });
  }
</PRE>
The Toolbar never knows what the buttons do: MainWindow gives it the actions. That keeps it a toolkit module
that any program can use.<P>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Pictures of the program's own</H2>
The buttons need pictures. A RISC OS program usually keeps its sprites in a sprite file in its application
directory, and !Contacts5 has two: <CODE>!Sprites</CODE>, with the application's icon, which !Run puts in
the Wimp's sprite pool with <CODE>IconSprites</CODE> so the Filer and the icon bar can show it (as the first book's
!Snake did), and <CODE>Sprites</CODE>, with the toolbar's pictures <CODE>tb_new</CODE>,
<CODE>tb_edit</CODE> and <CODE>tb_delete</CODE>. Double-click <CODE>Sprites</CODE> to look at them in
Paint - and to draw your own.<P>
The toolbar's pictures don't go into the Wimp's pool. The pool is shared by every program on the desktop, so
a name like <CODE>tb_new</CODE> could easily clash with another program's. Instead the program reads the file
into a <I>sprite area</I> of its own, which is what <CODE>!RunImage</CODE> now does before anything else:<P>
<PRE>
export default async function start(task, ctx) {
  const app = {
    task,
    area: sprites.spritesFromFile(await vfs.readFile(ctx.dir +
      '.Sprites')),                  // the toolbar's pictures
  };
  const book = new Book();
  for (const c of CONTACTS) book.add(c);
  new MainWindow(app, book);
}
</PRE>
<CODE>sprites.spritesFromFile</CODE> turns the bytes of a sprite file into a sprite area.
<CODE>ctx.dir</CODE> is the application's directory (the first book's chapter 12), so the file is found wherever the
application is copied. Reading the file takes time, so <CODE>start</CODE> is now an <CODE>async</CODE>
function and <CODE>await</CODE>s it.<P>
The Toolbar gives the area to its pane (<CODE>spriteArea: area</CODE>): an icon in the pane looks for its
sprite there first, and only then in the Wimp's pool.<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Passing the application around</H2>
Look at the last lines of <CODE>start</CODE>: MainWindow is now given <CODE>app</CODE>, not
<CODE>task</CODE>. <CODE>app</CODE> is a small object holding what belongs to the whole application rather
than to one window: the task, and now the sprite area.<P>
Why not just add the area as a third argument, <CODE>new MainWindow(task, book, area)</CODE>? Because the
list would keep growing: chapter 6 adds the user's sort order, and later chapters add more. Every new thing
would change the constructor, and every call of it. With one <CODE>app</CODE> object, new things go into
<CODE>app</CODE> and the constructor stays the same. MainWindow keeps <CODE>this.task = app.task</CODE>, so
the rest of its code doesn't change.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>The toolbar in the window</H2>
Here is MainWindow making its toolbar:<P>
<PRE>
    this.bar = new Toolbar(w, {
      area: app.area,
      buttons: [
        { name: 'new', sprite: 'tb_new', action: () =&gt; this.add() },
        { name: 'edit', sprite: 'tb_edit',
          action: () =&gt; this.edit() },
        { name: 'delete', sprite: 'tb_delete',
          action: () =&gt; this.remove() },
      ],
      search: {
        change: (text) =&gt; { this.search = text; this.refresh(); },
      },
      menu: (ev) =&gt; this.openMenu(ev),
    });
</PRE>
Each button is an object with a name, a sprite and an action. The actions are the ones the menu and the keys
already use - <CODE>add</CODE>, <CODE>edit</CODE> and <CODE>remove</CODE> - so all three ways of doing a
thing do exactly the same thing. <CODE>menu</CODE> means Menu clicked on the toolbar opens the window's
usual menu.<P>
The list must start below the toolbar, so the ListView is told how much room to leave at the top:<P>
<PRE>
    this.list = new ListView(w, {
      top: this.bar.height, key: (c) =&gt; c.id,
      columns: [
        { title: 'Name', width: 190, text: nameOf },
        { title: 'Phone', width: 130, text: phoneOf },
        { title: 'Email', width: 190, text: (c) =&gt; c.email },
        { title: 'Category', width: 90, text: (c) =&gt; c.category },
      ],
    });
    this.list.on('open', (c) =&gt; this.edit(c));
    this.list.on('menu', ({ ev }) =&gt; this.openMenu(ev));
    this.list.on('select', () =&gt; this.shade());
</PRE>
<CODE>top: this.bar.height</CODE> is the new part. The last line is new too: whenever the selection changes,
the ListView emits <CODE>'select'</CODE> and the window greys out the buttons that can't be used:<P>
<PRE>
  /** Grey out the tools that can't be used just now. */
  shade() {
    const n = this.list.selected().length;
    this.bar.shade('edit', n !== 1);
    this.bar.shade('delete', n === 0);
  }
</PRE>
It's the same rule as the menu in chapter 4 (Change for exactly one contact, Delete for at least one) but
buttons, unlike menus, are always on the screen, so they have to be changed at the moment the selection
changes, not worked out when they're drawn.<P>

<H3>Help for each button</H3>
Buttons with only pictures need explaining, and the Toolbar lets each one carry interactive help, shown by
!Help when the pointer is over it:<P>
<PRE>
{ name: 'new', sprite: 'tb_new', action: () =&gt; this.add(),
  help: 'Click SELECT to add a contact.' },
</PRE>
!Contacts5 leaves it out to keep the listing short; chapter 9 gives help to every button, menu entry and
window in the program.<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>A Find field</H2>
The toolbar also has a writable icon labelled <I>Find</I>, made by the <CODE>search</CODE> option. Whenever
its text changes - every key you type - the Toolbar calls the <CODE>change</CODE> function MainWindow gave
it, which keeps the text and refreshes the list:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ showing the book
  refresh() {
    // only the contacts whose names contain the Find text
    const s = this.search.toLowerCase();
    this.list.setItems(this.book.list().filter((c) =&gt;
      nameOf(c).toLowerCase().includes(s)));
    this.shade();
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>refresh</CODE> now shows only the contacts whose names contain the Find text.
<CODE>toLowerCase</CODE> on both sides means capitals don't matter: <I>smi</I> finds Smith. With the Find
field empty, every name contains the empty string, so every contact is shown. Escape in the Find field clears
it, and Return gives the keyboard back to the list.<P>
This first version only looks at names, and it does the looking in the window. In the next chapter that turns
out to be the wrong place.<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Trying it</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Run <CODE>!Contacts5</CODE>. The Change and Delete buttons are greyed out: nothing is selected.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Click a contact: they come to life. Press Ctrl-A: Change goes grey again (more than one is
selected), Delete doesn't.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Click in the Find field and type <CODE>ev</CODE>: only Evans, Carol is left. Press Escape to see
everyone again.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Drag the window by its title bar and resize it: the toolbar goes with it.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>A pane is a window the Wimp keeps in front of another and moves with it. Make it with
<CODE>flags: { pane: true }</CODE> and attach it with <CODE>win.attachPane(pane, { ... })</CODE>.<P>
<LI>The Toolbar module makes a pane of buttons from a list; the program gives it the actions, and greys
buttons out with <CODE>shade</CODE>.<P>
<LI>A program's own pictures belong in a sprite area of its own, read from its sprite file with
<CODE>sprites.spritesFromFile</CODE>, so their names can't clash with other programs'.<P>
<LI>Pass one <CODE>app</CODE> object holding the application's shared things, rather than a growing list of
arguments.<P>
<LI>Buttons are always visible, so shade them when the selection changes (the ListView's
<CODE>'select'</CODE> event); menus can work it out as they open.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Give the three buttons interactive help, and check it with !Help.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add a fourth button, labelled <I>All</I> instead of a picture, that selects every contact. (A
button can have <CODE>text</CODE> and a width <CODE>w</CODE> instead of a sprite.)<P>
<DT><DD>3  Show in the window's title how many contacts the Find field has found, for example
<I>Contacts (3 found)</I>, but just <I>Contacts</I> when the Find field is empty.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Make the Find field look at the company as well as the name. (Chapter 6 does this properly.)<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Add <CODE>help</CODE> to each button, for example <CODE>help: 'Click SELECT to delete the chosen
contacts.'</CODE> Run !Help (in Apps) and move the pointer over the buttons.<P>
<DT><DD>2  In the <CODE>buttons</CODE> list:
<PRE>
{ name: 'all', text: 'All', w: 44,
  action: () =&gt; this.list.selectAll() },
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  At the end of <CODE>refresh</CODE>:
<PRE>
const n = this.list.items.length;
this.win.setTitle(this.search ? `Contacts (${n} found)`
  : 'Contacts');
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  Check both in the filter:
<PRE>
this.list.setItems(this.book.list().filter((c) =&gt;
  nameOf(c).toLowerCase().includes(s) ||
  (c.company ?? '').toLowerCase().includes(s)));
</PRE>
</DL>

